@page "/packs"

<PageTitle>Packs · louis-agent</PageTitle>

<Band Id="top" Kicker="Packs">
    <h2 class="chrome">What an agent can do is a package.</h2>
    <p class="lede">A pack is tools and their guide. An agent has only the packs its program names, and a session only the toolsets it asks for, so its prompt never describes a tool it can't use.</p>
    <GraphView Spec="PacksGraph.Spec" Title="A pack, what it holds, and what a session loads from it" />
</Band>

<Band Id="general" Kicker="General tools">
    <CardDeck>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.files" Summary="The workspace's files, and documents as text.">
            <p>Read, write, search, copy, rename and delete in the workspace, and read PDF, Word and Excel as text.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.git" Summary="The repository's history, everything but the push.">
            <p>Status, diffs, branches, commits, merges and history. Pushing stays with you.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.dotnet" Summary="Build, test, run and restore .NET projects.">
            <p>Build, test, run and restore .NET projects, and list their packages.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.shell" Summary="Python, PowerShell and bash, one toolset each.">
            <p>Run Python, PowerShell and bash, a toolset each, so a session loads only the one it needs.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.web" Summary="Search, read a page, check a quote, convert money.">
            <p>Search, read a page, read one a browser has to draw, check a quote is really on its page, and convert money.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.paymo" Summary="Paymo time tracking.">
            <p>Paymo time tracking: time entries, tasks, projects and summaries.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.devops" Summary="Azure DevOps work items and sprints.">
            <p>Azure DevOps work items, sprints and team queries.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.docs" Summary="A project's own docs, read-only. The guide's pack.">
            <p>A project's own docs, read-only: list a folder, search a word, read a doc. The guide in this page's corner answers with it.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.triage" Summary="What a triage team records its work with.">
            <p>What an agent team triages a service's errors with: a role ends its work with a typed result, a case goes to a person, a run reads back, a department is configuration. The workflow that runs on it is <a href="/whats-coming">coming</a>.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.leads" Summary="One tool: it tells Louis a visitor wants a site with an agent.">
            <p>For the guide in this page's corner: it tells Louis that a visitor wants a website with an agent or a custom agent, once they said yes, by mail. Without the mail key the lead goes to the log, and the guide says so.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.site" Summary="One tool: it takes you to a page of the site, or to a part of it.">
            <p>For the guide in this page's corner: when the right page or area exists for what you ask, it opens it for you, scrolled to the part and with that part lit up for a moment, then says in one line what to look at. The tool checks the path is a page of this site and nothing else; the page itself moves the browser.</p>
        </Card>
    </CardDeck>
</Band>

<Band Id="cook" Kicker="The packs behind Puls's cook">
    <CardDeck Columns="4">
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.where-to-buy" Summary="A shop's branches near you, as a short table.">
            <p>Finds a shop's branches near you from OpenStreetMap, and hands the model a short table instead of a map to guess at.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.shop-prices" Summary="A shopping list, estimated from Stats SA's averages.">
            <p>Estimates a shopping list from Stats SA's public averages, in your province, and always says it's an estimate.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.recipes" Summary="Recipes from 45 South African sites that allow readers.">
            <p>Reads recipes from 45 South African sites that allow readers, and reads a page's own recipe data before its text.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="louis-agent.pack.calculator" Summary="Sums in code, not in the model's head.">
            <p>Works out sums in code, so a model hands over its arithmetic instead of doing it in its head.</p>
        </Card>
    </CardDeck>
</Band>

<Band Id="rules" Kicker="The rules a pack keeps">
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong>Only the contracts.</strong> A pack references the small contracts package, never the engine, so it can ship on its own. A console app runs one from a package with no engine in its dependencies.</li>
        <li><strong>No other pack.</strong> A pack never knows about another pack or an identity. Only a demo's own program knows them all.</li>
        <li><strong>The engine's guards, not its own.</strong> It asks the engine for a browser, a search and a fetcher, so every request stays inside the rules: no internal addresses, and a site's <code>robots.txt</code> obeyed.</li>
        <li><strong>What proves it sits apart.</strong> A pack's evals are in a project of their own, so you can take the tools without them.</li>
        <li><strong>Ready for MCP.</strong> A pack's tools and guide keep a shape that can be served over MCP as they are.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="band-note"><a href="/how-it-works">How a turn uses them</a></p>
</Band>
